     ==============================================================================================================
     |                                               NORMALITY TEST                                               |
     |                                 FOR THE DISTRIBUTION OF CYCLE KEFF VALUES                                  |
     |                                           USING THE KHI2 METHOD                                            |
     |                                       FOR EACH INDIVIDUAL ESTIMATOR                                        |
     |                                             in system initial                                              |
     ==============================================================================================================

     The Khi2 test, performed in MORET, tests the likelihood of the hypothesis according to which
     the cycle keff values must be statistically distributed according to a normal distribution if
     the sources are correctly placed in the fissile materials for each cycle start.

     The Khi2, calculated in MORET, compares the frequencies of the cycle keff values divided into 12 expected
     theoretical frequency classes (based on the assumption of an ideal gaussian distribution of
     the sample of cycle keff values).

     The Khi2 value:
     - is zero when the frequencies observed are equal to the expected frequencies, i.e.
       when there is absolute agreement between the observed distribution and the expected distribution;
     - increases with increasing difference between the observed and expected frequencies.

     For sufficiently large samples, on a normal distribution, the probability of the Khi2
     statistic obtained being greater than a value x is known and tabulated as a function of x.
     Although the probability of obtaining a high value of the Khi2 statistic is nonzero,
     the lowest values are the most probable.
     For example, there is only a 5% chance of this value exceeding 16.919 for a sample
     from a normal distribution.
     As we wait for the cycle keff distribution to be normal, we wait until the Khi2 statistic
     of the cycle keff sample is not "abnormally" high.
     We arbitrarily consider that values of the Khi2 statistic greater than 16.919 are
     "abnormally" high because there is less than a 5% chance that a sample from a normal
     distribution would lead to such values.

     A statistical test is a decision-making criterion to choose between two hypotheses:
     H0: the sample of cycle keff values comes from a gaussian distribution.
     H1: the sample of cycle keff values does not come from a gaussian distribution.
     It consists of setting an ARBITRARY threshold (5%), such that, when the probability associated with the
     value of the statistic:
     - is greater than the THRESHOLD, H0 is considered to be PLAUSIBLE (but not proven) and is therefore ACCEPTED;
     - is less than the THRESHOLD, H0 is considered to be IMPLAUSIBLE and is therefore REJECTED in favour of H1.
     There are two RISKS:
     - The risk ALPHA of wrongly rejecting the normality hypothesis (equal to the arbitrary 5% THRESHOLD);
     - the risk BETA of wrongly accepting the normality hypothesis.
     The lower the THRESHOLD (ALPHA), the higher BETA: no test is 100% reliable...
          ===================================================================================================
          |SUPPRESS.|    TEST   | SOURCE   | SOURCE   | SOURCE   | BALANCE  | BALANCE  | BALANCE  |NORMAL   |
          | CYCLES  |    KHI2   | COLL.    | TRACK    | ABSORP.  | COLL.    | TRACK    | ABSORP.  |ESTI.    |
          ===================================================================================================
          |       0 | Val. stat.|   2.545  |   9.327  |   6.726  |  10.544  |   6.420  |   5.663  |         |
          |         | Proba.    |  97.9 %  |  40.8 %  |  66.5 %  |  30.9 %  |  69.7 %  |  77.3 %  |         |
          |         | Normality | accepted | accepted | accepted | accepted | accepted | accepted |  6 / 6  |
          ===================================================================================================
          |      10 | Val. stat.|   3.438  |   4.430  |   7.947  |   5.228  |   5.644  |   6.719  |         |
          |         | Proba.    |  94.3 %  |  87.8 %  |  54.0 %  |  81.3 %  |  77.5 %  |  66.6 %  |         |
          |         | Normality | accepted | accepted | accepted | accepted | accepted | accepted |  6 / 6  |
          ===================================================================================================
